WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.080
The leaderboard's main number

2
00:00:02.142 --> 00:00:03.186
is the pass rate

3
00:00:03.186 --> 00:00:05.580
the share of a model's runs that pass.

4
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.352
With one run per task

5
00:00:07.352 --> 00:00:09.678
that is the number of tasks passed

6
00:00:09.678 --> 00:00:10.970
out of forty-eight.

7
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.800
Here are six of the sixteen rows.

8
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.884
Claude Opus 5.5 leads

9
00:00:15.884 --> 00:00:17.855
with twenty-eight tasks

10
00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:19.510
fifty-eight percent.

11
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.621
Gemini 4 Argon in Antigravity

12
00:00:22.716 --> 00:00:26.714
passed twenty-six, GPT-6 Astra twenty-one

13
00:00:26.714 --> 00:00:29.380
and GPT-6.1 Terra nineteen.

14
00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.299
Kimi K3.1 Pro passed twelve

15
00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:35.970
and Step 5 Preview four: eight percent.

16
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.275
Each task's evaluator also scores the final

17
00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:41.302
submission on the hidden suite

18
00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:42.950
from zero to one hundred.

19
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.329
A run that ends without a final

20
00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:46.900
submission scores zero.

21
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.379
The mean score chart averages each

22
00:00:49.441 --> 00:00:51.507
run's score minus the reference's

23
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.010
score on the same task.

24
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.320
So zero means level with the reference.

25
00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.846
Claude Opus 5.5 leads at

26
00:00:58.937 --> 00:01:00.750
plus nine point two.

27
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.860
Step 5 Preview is at minus ten point eight.

28
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.815
Pass rate treats every task the same

29
00:01:07.815 --> 00:01:10.640
but some tasks are much harder than others.

30
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.458
The FrontierCS Capabilities Index, or FCI

31
00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:16.606
applies the idea of Epoch

32
00:01:16.688 --> 00:01:18.670
AI's capabilities index.

33
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.836
One fit gives every model an ability

34
00:01:21.836 --> 00:01:25.260
and every task a difficulty, called its FDI.

35
00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.220
A model beats a task's checks more often

36
00:01:28.284 --> 00:01:30.066
the more its ability exceeds

37
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.530
the task's difficulty.

38
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.964
Here is the core of that idea

39
00:01:34.036 --> 00:01:36.911
on a toy table of our own: three models

40
00:01:36.911 --> 00:01:37.773
five tasks

41
00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:40.360
one for a pass and zero for a miss.

42
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.993
The chance that a model passes a

43
00:01:43.058 --> 00:01:45.239
task is the sigmoid of its ability

44
00:01:45.303 --> 00:01:47.100
minus the task's difficulty.

45
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.864
Gradient ascent finds the values that

46
00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:51.690
make the table most likely.

47
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.772
A small shrink term keeps them finite

48
00:01:54.772 --> 00:01:57.000
and Model Z is the zero point.

49
00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.394
Model A comes out at four

50
00:01:59.394 --> 00:02:01.830
and Model B at one point four.

51
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.704
Task four, which nobody passed

52
00:02:04.704 --> 00:02:06.740
is the hardest, at five.

53
00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.405
Task one, which everybody passed

54
00:02:09.405 --> 00:02:12.210
is the easiest, at minus one point two.

55
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.568
Tasks three and five were each

56
00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:16.174
passed only by Model A

57
00:02:16.174 --> 00:02:18.382
so they get the same difficulty

58
00:02:18.382 --> 00:02:19.520
two point seven.

59
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.900
The real fit is richer than the toy.

60
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.957
It models each metric a task checks

61
00:02:25.957 --> 00:02:27.010
and each run.

62
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.138
A task's FDI is then the strength at which

63
00:02:30.204 --> 00:02:32.320
a model passes it half the time.

64
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.589
The fit also counts how far each final score

65
00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.110
lands above or below the reference's.

66
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.373
Its zero point is Kimi K2.7

67
00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:42.627
which is not on the leaderboard

68
00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.359
and it reports ninety percent intervals

69
00:02:45.427 --> 00:02:47.340
from a bootstrap over tasks.

70
00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.226
On the real scale

71
00:02:49.226 --> 00:02:52.810
Claude Opus 5.5 has an FCI of fifty-four.

72
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.680
The hardest task is KV Compaction

73
00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.200
with an FDI of one hundred three.

74
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.626
Runs also differ in cost

75
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.211
an agent that tries more ideas can

76
00:03:03.285 --> 00:03:05.500
spend more on its model's API.

77
00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.859
A test-time scaling curve asks

78
00:03:07.924 --> 00:03:10.321
what would a model score if every run

79
00:03:10.386 --> 00:03:12.330
were stopped at a cost budget?

80
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.755
A run that ended within the budget

81
00:03:14.816 --> 00:03:16.280
counts what it achieved.

82
00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:18.529
A run still going at the budget

83
00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.428
counts as one that ended there

84
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.144
without a final submission

85
00:03:22.144 --> 00:03:23.430
so it beats nothing.

86
00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.763
That is why a curve can never

87
00:03:25.831 --> 00:03:27.540
fall as the budget grows.

88
00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:29.352
Here are six toy runs

89
00:03:29.352 --> 00:03:32.210
from under one dollar to about twenty-six.

90
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.305
At a budget of one dollar

91
00:03:34.305 --> 00:03:36.720
the pass rate is seventeen percent.

92
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.310
At five dollars, thirty-three.

93
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.500
At ten, fifty.

94
00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.170
At thirty, sixty-seven.

95
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.358
The real curves plot FCI

96
00:03:46.435 --> 00:03:48.120
rather than pass rate.

97
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.260
A full evaluation is expensive.

98
00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.077
For Claude Opus 5.5

99
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.597
one pass over the forty-eight tasks costs about

100
00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.310
one thousand US dollars in API fees

101
00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.290
three hundred in GPU time

102
00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:03.417
and one hundred in CPU time

103
00:04:03.417 --> 00:04:05.690
about fourteen hundred in all.

104
00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:06.707
The tasks

105
00:04:06.707 --> 00:04:09.573
their evaluators and the judge are public

106
00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:12.173
as one evaluation environment that you

107
00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.507
install in your own cloud account.

108
00:04:14.507 --> 00:04:17.240
GPU tasks also need a GPU cloud service.

109
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.740
That is how the benchmark works.

110
00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:22.730
Each later lecture covers one field.

111
00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.628
For every task, you will see the idea

112
00:04:25.628 --> 00:04:27.774
on a small example you can run

113
00:04:27.774 --> 00:04:30.524
what the agent writes; how it is judged

114
00:04:30.524 --> 00:04:32.200
and what the agents did.

115
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.288
Lecture 4, on networks

116
00:04:34.288 --> 00:04:36.630
is a good place to start: BBQ

117
00:04:36.706 --> 00:04:39.954
MetaOpt and Shockwave each show a different

118
00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.070
way a run can pass or fail.

119
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.287
As an exercise, suppose that a run passes

120
00:04:45.287 --> 00:04:47.540
and that it costs forty dollars.

121
00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.810
At which budgets does it count?

122
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.417
The interactive notes for this lecture

123
00:04:52.476 --> 00:04:54.080
have every program we used.

124
00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.217
Next time: Lecture 1

125
00:04:56.217 --> 00:04:58.430
machine-learning systems.
